<p>Gerardo Mérida Sánchez, the former public security secretary of Mexico’s Sinaloa state, appeared in federal court in Manhattan after his May 11 arrest in Arizona on U.S. charges alleging cartel ties, court records and officials said. Newsday and ABC News reported that he was not required to enter a plea at the Friday appearance. Mérida Sánchez is the first of 10 indicted Sinaloa officials or former officials to surrender to U.S. authorities in a New York case with reported counts including narcotics-importation conspiracy and firearms charges involving machine guns and destructive devices. Enrique Díaz Vega, Sinaloa’s former finance secretary, also surrendered to U.S. authorities, making him the second accused official in U.S. custody, El País reported, citing Reforma and official sources. Other defendants identified in the case include Sinaloa Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya and Culiacán Mayor Juan de Dios Gámez Mendívil.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6L4TuG3BCDhhvLb9w_MRZ68FnKFqz2ZxwZnUdZmHtBN9a2OuuopcwHWS_BnueWpBqkAFOfbaCiwrOFwpqMHXAihNCa25hNtfJ9KYi4iXpAlIUucHoJg4Mu6MusyVr3mwbcJzrDVMRPlrsY15u78WdmCTqqPISi9E3vtKohz48ZkXVXEri7ZMfe-dv6gfK4j9mmsD8eKyuSzM8JzqIjBmaTqzsogffdw97Z-BQSvpFcD1hmBT6Xka9f02zcgH2lGGCb9gAShZBUZHVW7HQThrzVLkK1uCpsDO' alt='Former Sinaloa security secretary Gerardo Mérida Sánchez.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Transfer procedure: W Radio reported that Mérida Sánchez did not contest identity proceedings before his transfer to New York, and said he was held in a Brooklyn prison.</li><li>Drug list: El Financiero reported that the alleged trafficking involved fentanyl, cocaine, heroin and methamphetamine shipments into the United States.</li><li>Political allegations: El Financiero reported that the indictment alleges Díaz Vega gave Los Chapitos the names and addresses of Rocha Moya’s opponents before the June 2021 Sinaloa governor’s election so they could be threatened into withdrawing.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mexico Security Cabinet: The cabinet said Mérida Sánchez crossed from Sonora into Arizona and that Mexican authorities kept formal information channels open with U.S. agencies through the Security Cabinet and the Foreign Ministry. (<a href='https://wradio.com.mx/2026/05/15/arrestan-en-eu-a-gerardo-merida-sanchez-de-que-acusan-al-exsecretario-de-seguridad-de-sinaloa/'>W Radio</a>)</li><li>U.S. prosecutors: Prosecutors allege Sinaloa officials received bribes, leaked official information and provided institutional and police protection for Los Chapitos in exchange for money, electoral support and favors. (<a href='https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/05/15/gerardo-merida-se-entrega-quienes-son-los-otros-funcionarios-mexicanos-acusados-por-eu/'>El Financiero</a>)</li><li>Rocha Moya and Gámez Mendívil: The Sinaloa governor and Culiacán mayor, both named among the defendants, said they were taking temporary leaves of absence to deal with the U.S. charges. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/ex-sinaloa-security-chief-first-of-10-indicted-mexican-officials-to-surrender-to-us-authorities/article70985884.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Mexican federal government: El País reported that President Claudia Sheinbaum’s government has repeatedly asked Washington for evidence before determining whether any detention or extradition request in Mexico should proceed. (<a href='https://elpais.com/mexico/2026-05-16/el-cerco-se-estrecha-sobre-rocha-moya-estados-unidos-ya-tiene-en-su-poder-a-sus-responsables-de-seguridad-y-finanzas.html'>El País</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/former-mexican-governors-ally-in-us-custody-on-charges-of-cartel-ties'>Allies of Mexico&#x27;s Sinaloa governor in US custody on alleged drug charges</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/ex-sinaloa-security-chief-first-of-10-indicted-mexican-officials-to-surrender-to-us-authorities/article70985884.ece'>Ex-Sinaloa 